        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objectives:</B><P>The aim of the experiment was to evaluate the effects of microgravity on fine structure and protein organization of choroidal cells after a space flight. The functional consequences of spaceflight and hindlimb-suspension were evaluated by immunocytochemistry and by molecular biology by detecting changes in the expression of cytoskeletal and membrane proteins in choroidal cells which produce cerebrospinal fluid. Moreover, qualitative changes in the biosynthesis and storage of natriuretic peptides (using electron microscopy, immunocytochemistry) in hypothalamus and heart were evaluated under microgravity conditions.<P><B>Approach:</B><P>Rat brains were removed after decapitation and choroid plexuses from 5 brains dissected and fixed inflight and 3 others dissected 5-8 hours after landing were carefully isolated, briefly rinsed in PBS, and fixed by immersion in 2.5 percent glutaraldehyde in cacodylate buffer  0.1M, pH 7.4, post-fixed in 1 percent osmium tetroxide in sodium barbital-acetate buffer 0.05M, pH 7.4 and embedded in LX-112 epoxy resin. Those from 2 other animals dissected 5-8 hours after landing were fixed in the same conditions in 3% paraformaldehyde in PBS. Hypothalamus and the remaining brain with brainstem from these same brains were removed and directly frozen on dry ice, before storage at -80 degrees C. and shipping on dry ice to the lab, where they were stored at -80 degrees C until sectioning. Whole brains from 2 animals in each group were immersed in Bouinï¿½s fixative for 16 h, washed and dehydrated in ethanol, before embedding in Paraplast. Four other whole brains were directly frozen on dry ice, and stored at -80 degrees C. Hearts were similarly processed for electron microscopy, immunocytochemistry. Atrial and ventricle samples were frozen for radioimmunoassay and northern blot.<P><B>Results:</B><P>Ultrastructural observations of choroidal cells dissected inflight or 5-8 h postflight from adult rats showed a loss of cell polarity (altered kinocilia, loss of microvilli, decreases in apical ezrin) and a reduced choroidal secretion (accumulation of apical vesicles, loss of apical membrane and cytoplasmic molecules, involved in water and ionic transports, such as aquaporin 1, Na/K ATPase, carbonic anhydrase II). Similar effects were observed in hindlimb-suspended rats, whereas control rats displayed typical choroidal features. Those results suggested that, after 2 weeks in weightlessness, choroidal functions were altered, indicating a reduction in the secretory processes. As cerebrospinal fluid is mainly produced by these cells, we concluded that spaceflight and head-down suspension probably induce a reduced CSF production. Analyses of natriuretic peptides in hypothalamus and heart are still in progress.
        </div>

